Yes, partly. The closest option in the excerpts is a `startup` event handler. You register it with `@app.on_event("startup")`, and it runs before the application starts [1]:

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI

app = FastAPI()

items = {}

@app.on_event("startup")
async def startup_event():
    items["foo"] = {"name": "Fighters"}
    items["bar"] = {"name": "Tenders"}
```

You can add more than one such handler, and the app won't receive requests until all of them have completed [1].

The excerpts don't show code running after startup has fully finished. They also don't cover shutdown logic. The `startup` event section is labeled "(deprecated)" in its heading [1], but the excerpts don't name a replacement.
